section(r'\begin{abstract}',r'\end{abstract}',r'''\begin{abstract}
Group memory can carry part of a temporary correction's effect beyond its withdrawal. We examine this response in a three-state spreading model with prescribed synthetic rates and continuously relaxing signed memories on higher-order groups. Paired continuations start from the same treated node configuration at withdrawal, either keeping its memory or replacing it with memory from an untreated history. This comparison separates the response stored in node states from the additional response stored in groups. At the reference duration, the memory component accounts for 4.51\% of the mean loss reduction on synthetic networks, and 19.19\% and 6.43\% on sensor-derived high-school and primary-school contact topologies. An exact common-event construction combines the three-state node order with reverse numerical memory order. It gives pathwise comparisons, conservative fluctuation bounds and intersection--union bounds for nonnested interventions, even when node labels alone are not Markovian. A complete three-node example exhibits strict pair complementarity and a zero submodularity ratio. Numerical controls distinguish the decay of initial memory from the feedback generated by later group activity: a smaller initial-memory response can coexist with a larger spreading burden. The results describe finite-time relaxation and intervention response. Shared evaluation of candidate interventions preserves their joint trajectory distribution and reduces computation in the reported workloads.
''')

section(r'\section{Introduction}',r'\section{Memory-dependent competing spreading}',r'''\section{Introduction}
A temporary correction changes who transmits a rumor, but may also change the influence stored in the groups through which it spreads. These two changes need not relax on the same time scale. Once the external input ends, identical node configurations can respond differently because their groups carry different histories. Higher-order reinforcement makes this distinction relevant: several active donors are needed to open a transmission channel, and the group's past activity can modulate its strength~\cite{iacopini2019,battiston2020,battiston2021}.

Three questions arise when group memory is a dynamical variable. How much of the post-withdrawal response is carried by the memory produced during correction? Prescribing opposite initial memories reveals history sensitivity, but does not isolate this contribution. Does a stronger correction always reduce spreading, and does that monotone response imply diminishing returns? The latter property underlies classical influence guarantees~\cite{kempe2003,nemhauser1978}, while donor reinforcement permits complementary interventions. Finally, how can several candidate corrections be evaluated without changing the stochastic dependence on which their comparison relies?

We address these questions in a recurrent rumor--inactive--correction model. Each filled triangle carries a signed memory that relaxes toward its current majority. Correction acts for a finite duration on selected nodes, and the observable is cumulative rumor occupation after withdrawal. The continued-influence effect motivates separating current labels from persisting endorsement~\cite{lewandowsky2012}; the present model represents this distinction at group level through specified transition rates. Synthetic networks and sensor-derived high-school and primary-school contact groups provide the topologies for the numerical study~\cite{benson2018,highschool2015,primaryschool2011}.

The paper makes three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item A paired withdrawal-time comparison decomposes the intervention response into node-state and memory components. Memory controls and a relaxation-rate scan then distinguish a stored initial bias from feedback generated by subsequent activity.
\item A compatible order on node states and signed memory gives exact pathwise comparisons and bounds for nested and nonnested interventions. A complete-process counterexample separates this monotonicity from diminishing returns.
\item An exact evaluator shares event computation across candidate interventions while preserving their joint trajectory distribution. Its application is assessed through error, variance and computation cost, with the extended search studies reported in the supplement.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to previous work}
Higher-order contagion changes the donor requirements of pairwise spreading and can produce collective activation~\cite{iacopini2019,neuhauser2020}. Reviews place these models in the broader study of network structure and dynamics~\cite{battiston2020,battiston2021,bick2023,boccaletti2023}. Temporal contacts and path-dependent network models encode other forms of history~\cite{holme2012,lambiotte2019}. Here the topology is fixed and a continuous variable on each group evolves between node transitions. The empirical contact groups are the timestamped simplices assembled for simplicial-closure analysis~\cite{benson2018}; they originate from sensor time windows, rather than from filling triangles in an aggregate graph.

Common-random-map constructions are established tools for attractive multitype processes~\cite{stover2010}. Our augmented state is a piecewise-deterministic Markov process~\cite{davis1984}, whose exact marginal simulation uses reaction events and thinning~\cite{gillespie1977,lewis1979}. Common reaction paths supply another coupling~\cite{anderson2007,anderson2012}. Stacked coupling handles time-dependent intensities~\cite{andersonyuan2019}; Anderson and Ma's recent author preprint considers finite collections of reaction-network paths~\cite{andersonma2026}. The additional step here is to verify compatibility between the node order, the reverse order on signed memory and the continuous relaxation flow. This check supplies sufficient conditions for comparing the complete state--memory process and extends to weighted factors and heterogeneous relaxation.

Complementarity is also distinct from monotone response. Threshold diffusion and higher-order seeding need not satisfy diminishing returns~\cite{schoenebeck2022,zhang2024,qu2025}, and weak-submodularity guarantees require a positive ratio~\cite{das2018}. Our triangle example tests this requirement in the full recurrent dynamics, including forgetting, temporary control and memory relaxation. Influence-blocking heuristics and search certificates serve as supplementary applications of the comparison framework.
''')

