section(r'\section{Discussion}',r'\section{Conclusion}',r'''\section{Discussion}
The withdrawal comparison distinguishes two ways a temporary correction persists: it changes the node configuration and the influence stored in groups. Holding the treated node state fixed isolates the second component under the specified pairing. Its positive value on the tested synthetic and contact-topology ensembles shows that intervention-generated memory can carry a measurable part of the later response. The reference matters physically. On both contact topologies, retaining treated memory improves on restoring untreated memory, while complete erasure can improve further. Correction can therefore leave groups more favorable than the untreated baseline without making their remaining memory wholly favorable.

Along a realized node path,
\[
 A_\sigma(t)=e^{-\lambda t}A_\sigma(0)
 +\lambda\int_0^t e^{-\lambda(t-s)}\Phi_\sigma(X(s))\,ds.
\]
The first term forgets the initial state; the second stores subsequent group activity. Increasing $\lambda$ changes both processes: memory loses its initial bias faster and follows the contemporary drive more closely. This explains why the shrinking initial-memory gap in Fig.~\ref{fig:memory_timescales} can coexist with an increasing occupation loss in Fig.~\ref{fig:natural_memory}. The triangle map separates direct retention, sampled within-path rebound and overshoot of the mean curve. They measure different responses of a finite stochastic system.

The comparison theory makes these experiments tractable without closing the dynamics at the level of node-state means. Its assumptions are checkable sufficient conditions on the drive, reset rates and memory flow. Weighted four-uniform examples illustrate the extension, including a linear count drive. State-dependent forgetting, control-dependent relaxation and redistribution of a fixed total intervention intensity require separate compatibility checks. The fluctuation bounds are conservative; their empirical slack describes the remaining variability rather than a test of population coverage.

The results concern prescribed synthetic dynamics and finite observation windows. Sensor-derived groups supply topology, with simulation uncertainty conditional on two histories per dataset. The paired memory fraction is specific to the chosen untreated-memory reference and coupling. No phase boundary or critical point is inferred. Optimization has a further structural limit: strict pair complementarity excludes a uniform positive submodularity ratio, while search and computational gains depend on the candidate sets, budget and implementation. The supplement reports those comparisons, including uncertain outcomes and mixed transfer.
''')

section(r'\section{Conclusion}',r'\section*{CRediT',r'''\section{Conclusion}
A temporary correction can leave a response in group memory as well as in node states. Paired continuations at withdrawal separate these components, and the synthetic-network and contact-topology experiments quantify the memory contribution while distinguishing initial-memory decay from subsequent feedback. A compatible node--memory order gives exact pathwise comparisons, conservative fluctuation bounds and intersection--union envelopes for nonnested interventions. Strict pair complementarity on one triangle shows that this monotonicity does not imply diminishing returns or a uniform positive submodularity ratio. Shared event evaluation preserves the joint trajectory distribution used in these comparisons, with computation and search serving as supporting applications.
''')

